BRIGG.AI V1 — TERMINAL & ML CENTER UPDATE

Live Signal Layer, Interactive Terminal Guide, Advanced Chart, Token Lifecycle & ML Learning Center

IMPORTANT IMPLEMENTATION RULE

This update must extend the existing Brigg.AI application. Do not redesign the existing Terminal or ML Center from scratch.

Preserve the current functionality, layout, ML terminology, scoring system, model versioning, validation gates, audit logs, token scanner, chart data pipeline, and existing Action State behavior unless a requirement below explicitly changes them.

The existing Brigg application already contains concepts such as:

- HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY
- WAIT
- NO TRADE
- Opportunity Score
- Risk Score
- Confidence
- Execution Quality
- Calibrated Probability
- Market Regime
- Why Opportunity Exists
- Pump/Dump detection
- ML model versions
- Training samples
- New observations
- Feature versions
- Out-of-Time Validation
- ROC-AUC
- PR-AUC
- F1 / Precision
- Brier Score
- False Positive Rate
- Sharpe / Profit Factor
- Model promotion
- Model rollback

These existing concepts must remain functional.

---

PART 1 — TERMINAL UPDATE

1. OPTIONAL LIVE SIGNAL LAYER

Add a new Live Signal layer to the Terminal.

This must be a separate layer from the existing ML Action State.

DO NOT replace the existing Action State.

The current Action State must continue displaying classifications such as:

- HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY
- WAIT
- NO TRADE
- other existing ML classifications

For example, the existing Terminal may continue showing:

ACTION STATE

HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY

Opportunity       77/100
Risk              29/100
Confidence        72%
Execution Quality 86/100

Do not convert:

HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY

into:

BUY

These are different concepts.

---

2. LIVE SIGNAL MUST BE AN OPTIONAL USER-CONTROLLED FEATURE

Add a separate Live Signal feature that users can turn ON or OFF.

Default behavior should preserve the existing Terminal appearance if the feature is disabled.

Setting

Add:

Settings
→ Terminal
→ Live Signal

with:

Live Trading Signal       [ ON / OFF ]

Additional controls:

Show Signal on Action Panel     [ ON / OFF ]
Show Signal on Chart            [ ON / OFF ]
Show Signal Confidence          [ ON / OFF ]
Show Signal Explanation         [ ON / OFF ]

When Live Signal is OFF:

Do not show BUY/SELL/PARTIAL SELL/HOLD on the Terminal or chart.

The existing Action State remains exactly as before.

---

3. LIVE SIGNAL TYPES

When Live Signal is enabled, Brigg may display:

BUY
HOLD
PARTIAL SELL
SELL

The signal must be generated from current market conditions and the existing Brigg analysis/ML pipeline.

It must not simply translate:

HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY → BUY

or:

WAIT → SELL

The Live Signal must be an independent decision layer.

---

4. LIVE SIGNAL EXAMPLE

Example:

ACTION STATE

HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY

Opportunity       77/100
Risk              29/100
Confidence        72%
Execution Quality 86/100

────────────────────────

LIVE SIGNAL

BUY

Signal Confidence: 78%

Another valid state:

ACTION STATE

HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY

Opportunity       77/100
Risk              29/100

────────────────────────

LIVE SIGNAL

HOLD

Signal Confidence: 64%

Another:

ACTION STATE

HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY

Opportunity       77/100
Risk              29/100

────────────────────────

LIVE SIGNAL

PARTIAL SELL

Signal Confidence: 82%

The existing Action State and the Live Signal must be allowed to show different information because they represent different analytical layers.

---

5. LIVE SIGNAL MUST UPDATE WITH LIVE MARKET DATA

The Live Signal must be dynamic.

Whenever new valid market observations arrive, Brigg should reevaluate the current signal.

Relevant inputs may include the existing Brigg features such as:

- momentum
- volume
- liquidity
- wallet activity
- social signals
- execution conditions
- market regime
- buy/sell flow
- price movement
- volatility
- support/resistance
- other validated features already available to the Brigg model

The signal should not update randomly or on a fake timer.

It must respond to actual changes in the underlying market data.

---

6. SIGNAL TIMESTAMP

Every Live Signal should have a timestamp or update indicator.

Example:

LIVE SIGNAL
BUY

Confidence: 78%
Updated: 12:41:08

When the market changes:

LIVE SIGNAL
HOLD

Confidence: 65%
Updated: 12:41:18

The UI should make it clear that the signal is live.

---

7. LIVE SIGNAL EXPLANATION

When enabled, provide a concise explanation of why the current signal exists.

Example:

LIVE SIGNAL

BUY

Confidence: 78%

Why:
+ Buy flow increasing
+ Momentum improving
+ Liquidity remains healthy
+ Price above short-term trend
− Volatility remains elevated

The explanation must be based on the actual features/data supporting the current signal.

Do not generate generic explanations that are disconnected from the model.

---

8. LIVE SIGNAL ON CHART

If:

Show Signal on Chart = ON

display signal markers directly on the live chart.

Examples:

BUY
  ↓
🟢

PARTIAL SELL
      ↓
     🟠

SELL
 ↓
🔴

Markers must contain timestamp information.

Example:

BUY
12:42:18

The chart should preserve the existing chart functionality.

---

9. DO NOT OVERLOAD THE CHART

The existing screenshot already contains multiple chart annotations such as:

- PUMP IN
- PUMP OUT
- DUMP OUT
- price labels
- support
- resistance
- indicators
- volume
- other objects

Do not make the chart unreadable by adding excessive Live Signal labels.

Implement:

Chart Settings
→ AI Overlay

with controls for:

Live Signal
AI Explanation
Pump/Dump Labels
Support/Resistance
ML Annotations
Confidence Labels

Each can be independently shown or hidden where technically appropriate.

