PART 7 — ML SAFETY / VALIDATION

30. PRESERVE THE EXISTING MODEL VALIDATION GATE

Do not allow:

NEW DATA
   ↓
TRAIN
   ↓
AUTOMATIC PRODUCTION

Instead maintain a controlled pipeline:

NEW DATA
   ↓
DATA VALIDATION
   ↓
FEATURE GENERATION
   ↓
TRAINING
   ↓
OUT-OF-TIME VALIDATION
   ↓
METRIC CHECK
   ↓
VALIDATION GATE
   ↓
CANDIDATE MODEL
   ↓
COMPARE WITH PRODUCTION
   ↓
PROMOTE / REJECT
   ↓
PRODUCTION

---

31. MODEL ROLLBACK

Preserve the existing model audit log and rollback capability.

Every promoted model should retain:

- model version
- date
- feature version
- training sample count
- validation metrics
- reason for promotion
- previous production version
- rollback availability

Do not remove the current rollback mechanism.

---

32. NO FAKE ML CLAIMS

Brigg must distinguish between:

Actual ML event

and:

UI explanation

Do not display that Brigg “learned” something unless an actual learning/training/validation event occurred.

Do not fabricate:

- training samples
- model improvements
- observations
- validation metrics
- model versions
- feature importance
- confidence
- calibration
- learning events

All displayed ML information must be traceable to the actual Brigg data/model pipeline.

---

33. FINAL UX PRINCIPLE

The Terminal must remain understandable for beginners while still providing advanced information for technical users.

The design should follow:

BEGINNER
   ↓
Simple Explanation
   ↓
Visual Guide
   ↓
Technical Detail
   ↓
Raw ML Information

Do not remove technical information merely to make the UI simpler.

Instead provide:

Simple Mode
+
Technical Mode

---

34. FINAL FUNCTIONAL RELATIONSHIP

The final Brigg architecture should conceptually work like this:

                    LIVE MARKET DATA
                           │
                           ↓
                    DATA PROCESSING
                           │
                           ↓
                    FEATURE ENGINE
                           │
             ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
             ↓                           ↓
       ML ANALYSIS                LIVE SIGNAL ENGINE
             │                           │
             ↓                           ↓
      ACTION STATE                 BUY / HOLD /
   HIGH-RISK OPPORTUNITY          PARTIAL SELL / SELL
      WAIT / NO TRADE
             │                           │
             └─────────────┬─────────────┘
                           ↓
                    TERMINAL UI
                           │
             ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
             ↓                           ↓
          CHART                      ACTION PANEL
             │
             ↓
       OPTIONAL AI OVERLAY
             │
             ↓
       TERMINAL GUIDE

The Action State and Live Signal are intentionally separate.

The existing Action State remains the primary Brigg classification.

The Live Signal is an optional additional layer that users can enable when they want real-time directional signals.

